### What is ThreadLocal?

ThreadLocal<T> provides thread-confined storage. Each thread that accesses the variable gets its own independent copy. No synchronization is needed because no two threads share the same value.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ThreadLocalBasic {
    private static final ThreadLocal<String> currentUser = new ThreadLocal<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            currentUser.set("Rajesh");
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                + ": " + currentUser.get());
        }, "teller-1");

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            currentUser.set("Priya");
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                + ": " + currentUser.get());
        }, "teller-2");

        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join();  t2.join();
    }
}
```

```text
teller-1: Rajesh
teller-2: Priya
```

### Why is ThreadLocal dangerous in thread pools?

In a thread pool, worker threads are reused across many tasks. A ThreadLocal value set by one task persists when the next task runs on the same thread. This causes a data leak: the next task reads stale data from a previous task.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ThreadLocalLeakDemo {
    private static final ThreadLocal<String> currentUser = new ThreadLocal<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(1); // one thread

        // Task 1: sets ThreadLocal for Rajesh
        pool.submit(() -> {
            currentUser.set("Rajesh");
            System.out.println("Task-1 user: " + currentUser.get());
            // FORGOT to remove()!
        }).get();

        // Task 2: runs on the SAME worker thread
        pool.submit(() -> {
            String user = currentUser.get();
            System.out.println("Task-2 user: " + user);
            // Reads "Rajesh" even though Task-2 never set it!
            // If this is Priya's request, it runs with Rajesh's context.
        }).get();

        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
Task-1 user: Rajesh
Task-2 user: Rajesh
```

### How does the memory leak happen?

ThreadLocal values are stored in a `ThreadLocalMap` inside each `Thread` object. The map key is a `WeakReference<ThreadLocal>`, but the value is a strong reference. In a pool, threads live forever, so the ThreadLocalMap entries are never cleaned up even if the ThreadLocal variable itself goes out of scope.

```text
Thread.threadLocals (ThreadLocalMap)
  Key: WeakReference<ThreadLocal>  →  may be GC'd
  Value: STRONG reference to stored object  →  NOT GC'd!

In a pool:
  Thread lives forever → map entries never cleaned
  200 threads x 1000 tasks x 1MB per value = memory leak
```

### How do you fix the problem?

Always call `threadLocal.remove()` in a finally block after every task.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ThreadLocalSafe {
    private static final ThreadLocal<String> currentUser = new ThreadLocal<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(1);

        // CORRECT: remove() in finally
        pool.submit(() -> {
            try {
                currentUser.set("Rajesh");
                System.out.println("Task-1 user: " + currentUser.get());
            } finally {
                currentUser.remove(); // ALWAYS!
            }
        }).get();

        pool.submit(() -> {
            String user = currentUser.get();
            System.out.println("Task-2 user: " + user); // null, correct!
        }).get();

        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
Task-1 user: Rajesh
Task-2 user: null
```

### What is the safe wrapper pattern?

Create a utility that wraps any Runnable with automatic ThreadLocal cleanup.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.List;

public class ThreadLocalCleaner {
    private final List<ThreadLocal<?>> locals;

    public ThreadLocalCleaner(ThreadLocal<?>... locals) {
        this.locals = List.of(locals);
    }

    public Runnable wrap(Runnable task) {
        return () -> {
            try {
                task.run();
            } finally {
                locals.forEach(ThreadLocal::remove);
            }
        };
    }
}
```

### What about InheritableThreadLocal?

InheritableThreadLocal copies the parent thread's value to child threads at creation time. In a pool, the child threads are reused, not newly created. So the inherited value is stale: it reflects whatever parent happened to create the pool thread, not the current parent. The same remove() fix applies.

### What about Spring's RequestContextHolder?

Spring stores the current HttpServletRequest in a ThreadLocal via `RequestContextHolder`. Spring's DispatcherServlet cleans it up in its own finally block. But if you create custom ThreadLocals for request-scoped data, you are responsible for calling remove() yourself.

### What are common mistakes with ThreadLocal in pools?

```java
// WRONG: no remove()
pool.submit(() -> {
    currentUser.set("Rajesh");
    processTransaction();
    // next task on this thread sees "Rajesh"!
});

// WRONG: remove() not in finally
pool.submit(() -> {
    currentUser.set("Rajesh");
    processTransaction(); // if this throws, remove() is skipped!
    currentUser.remove();
});

// CORRECT: remove() in finally
pool.submit(() -> {
    try {
        currentUser.set("Rajesh");
        processTransaction();
    } finally {
        currentUser.remove(); // runs even on exception
    }
});
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the internal data structure of ThreadLocal? Each Thread has a ThreadLocalMap where keys are WeakReferences to ThreadLocal instances and values are the stored objects.
- Why are keys weak references? So that when the ThreadLocal variable is no longer referenced, the key can be garbage collected and the entry can be lazily removed.
- Does remove() prevent both data leaks and memory leaks? Yes, remove() clears the entry from the ThreadLocalMap entirely.
